GNU Bison for OpenVMS
=====================

This is GNU Bison @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified GNU release plus the patches in the vms-bison project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-bison

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  Bison is free software under the GNU General
Public License, version 3 or later; see COPYING.  Parsers that Bison
generates may be used under the terms of the Bison exception in the
generated files.  The manual is BISON.TXT; BISON.1 is the manual page;
CALC.Y is a small example grammar.

Bison needs GNU m4 at run time: the M4 kit (https://github.com/issinoho/vms-m4)
is a prerequisite.

What is installed
-----------------
    BISON$ROOT:[BIN]BISON.EXE           bison
    BISON$ROOT:[DATA...]                skeletons and m4 library bison uses
    BISON$ROOT:[000000]BISON$SETUP.COM  defines the bison command
    BISON$ROOT:[DOC]                    this file, COPYING, NEWS, BISON.TXT,
                                        BISON.1, CALC.Y
    SYS$STARTUP:BISON$STARTUP.COM       defines BISON$ROOT

Setting up
----------
The kit installs BISON$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define BISON$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM, after the one for M4$STARTUP.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:BISON$STARTUP.COM

Each user (or SYLOGIN.COM, for everyone) then defines the command:

    $ @BISON$ROOT:[000000]BISON$SETUP.COM

Using bison
-----------
    $ bison --defines -o calc.c calc.y
    $ cc calc.c
    $ link calc

bison runs m4 as M4$ROOT:[BIN]M4.EXE and reads its data files from
BISON$ROOT:[DATA]; the logical names (or environment variables) M4 and
BISON_PKGDATADIR override them.  m4 runs in a subprocess, with its input
and output in temporary files in SYS$SCRATCH.

Upper-case options and batch jobs
---------------------------------
Under the TRADITIONAL DCL parse style DCL upper-cases the unquoted parts of
a command and the C run-time library passes them to bison in lower case,
so an unquoted -H (header) arrives as -h (help) and -L as -l.  Batch jobs
use TRADITIONAL even when interactive logins are set to EXTENDED.  Use the
long options (--defines, --language=c++), quote the short ones ("-H"), or
start the procedure with

    $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED

Exit status
-----------
Under DCL a failed run exits with an error-severity status, so ON ERROR and
IF .NOT. $STATUS work; bison's exit code N is ($STATUS .AND. %X7F8) / 8.
Under a GNV shell, $? is the exit code as on Unix.
